  [1mSection 2: Error handling and testing[0m

  [1mError handling[0m
  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m can throw an error: when storage is disabled, in some private-browsing modes, or when it's full.
    [38;5;153msession.js[39m catches these errors:
    - [38;5;153mgetCurrentUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m, so the app treats the user as logged out instead of crashing.
    - [38;5;153msetCurrentUser()[39m and [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser()[39m print a [38;5;153mconsole.warn[39m and carry on. The login itself still succeeds; it
      just won't be remembered after a reload.
  - [38;5;153msetCurrentUser()[39m refuses empty IDs ([38;5;153mnull[39m, [38;5;153mundefined[39m, [38;5;153m""[39m) and does nothing for them, so a failed login can't save a
    bad value.
  - A failed login ([38;5;153msuccess: false[39m) never calls [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m, and it doesn't clear an existing session either.

  [1mTesting[0m
  - There's no test setup yet. I'd use [1mNode's built-in test runner[0m ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m), which needs no packages, and add
    [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mtest/session.test.js[0m replaces [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with a simple in-memory fake and checks:
    - saving then reading returns the ID
    - reading with nothing saved returns [38;5;153mnull[39m
    - clearing removes the ID
    - empty IDs aren't saved
    - when storage throws, reads return [38;5;153mnull[39m and writes don't crash
  - [1mOne small reorganization so [38;5;153mlogin[39m can be tested:[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m grabs the form from the page as soon as it loads, so a test
    can't import it without a browser. I'd move [38;5;153mlogin[39m and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m into a new [1m[38;5;153mauth.js[0m. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps only the form
    handling and imports from [38;5;153mauth.js[39m. [1m[38;5;153mtest/auth.test.js[0m then checks that [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns the [38;5;153muserId[39m and that [38;5;153mvalidateForm
[39m    behaves as it does now.
